{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"R-48:26\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"48:25 restrains punishment where believers are hidden inside a hostile group; that is a useful analogue for 18:86’s قومًا, whom the following verses divide into a wrongdoer and a believer before assigning punishment or goodness.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"48:25\",\"ar\":\"وَلَوْلَا رِجَالٌۭ مُّؤْمِنُونَ وَنِسَآءٌۭ مُّؤْمِنَٰتٌۭ لَّمْ تَعْلَمُوهُمْ أَن تَطَـُٔوهُمْ فَتُصِيبَكُم مِّنْهُم مَّعَرَّةٌۢ بِغَيْرِ عِلْمٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"48:25\",\"ar\":\"لَوْ تَزَيَّلُوا۟ لَعَذَّبْنَا ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ مِنْهُمْ عَذَابًا أَلِيمًا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:87\",\"ar\":\"أَمَّا مَن ظَلَمَ فَسَوْفَ نُعَذِّبُهُۥ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:88\",\"ar\":\"وَأَمَّا مَنْ ءَامَنَ وَعَمِلَ صَٰلِحًۭا فَلَهُۥ جَزَآءً ٱلْحُسْنَىٰ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus finds a collective, then offers two forms of treatment; 18:87–88 make the distinction depend on the people’s conduct.\",\"limits\":\"This does not establish that believers were hidden among Dhu al-Qarnayn’s people or that the passages deliberately echo one another.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-49:9\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"49:9 pairs force against a transgressing party with a return to just reconciliation; it parallels the focus’s bounded choice between punishment and goodness, which the next verses assign by moral category.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"49:9\",\"ar\":\"فَإِنۢ بَغَتْ إِحْدَىٰهُمَا عَلَى ٱلْأُخْرَىٰ فَقَٰتِلُوا۟ ٱلَّتِى تَبْغِى حَتَّىٰ تَفِىٓءَ إِلَىٰٓ أَمْرِ ٱللَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"49:9\",\"ar\":\"فَإِن فَآءَتْ فَأَصْلِحُوا۟ بَيْنَهُمَا بِٱلْعَدْلِ وَأَقْسِطُوا۟\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:87\",\"ar\":\"أَمَّا مَن ظَلَمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s paired options and the next verse’s restriction of punishment to من ظلم make measured authority salient.\",\"limits\":\"49:9 concerns a conflict between believing factions and explicitly requires reconciliation; it does not prescribe Dhu al-Qarnayn’s particular policy.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-50:23\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-50:39\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-51:36\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The Lot narrative describes believers being taken out before the remaining Muslim household is identified; it offers a thin comparison for the focus’s differentiated treatment of a people.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"51:35\",\"ar\":\"فَأَخْرَجْنَا مَن كَانَ فِيهَا مِنَ ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"51:36\",\"ar\":\"فَمَا وَجَدْنَا فِيهَا غَيْرَ بَيْتٍۢ مِّنَ ٱلْمُسْلِمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:87\",\"ar\":\"أَمَّا مَن ظَلَمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:88\",\"ar\":\"وَأَمَّا مَنْ ءَامَنَ وَعَمِلَ صَٰلِحًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus moves from finding قومًا to distinguishing among them by conduct in 18:87–88.\",\"limits\":\"The Lot passage concerns rescue from a destroyed town and does not establish that Dhu al-Qarnayn’s people had the same composition.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-53:15\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-55:5\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-55:17\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The two westward points in 55:17 place the focus’s مغرب الشمس within a broader divine ordering of horizons; the singular reached west may be a route endpoint rather than a cosmic limit.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"55:17\",\"ar\":\"رَبُّ ٱلْمَشْرِقَيْنِ وَرَبُّ ٱلْمَغْرِبَيْنِ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ مَغْرِبَ ٱلشَّمْسِ\"}],\"missing\":\"The verses alone do not say what the two wests denote or how they relate to the traveler’s reached sunset place. Lexical or exegetical evidence about 55:17’s dual form could establish whether this contrast should shape the focus’s spatial reading.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-55:50\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"In 55:50, عينان are flowing springs in a garden scene; the focus instead qualifies its عين as حمئة and locates the sun’s setting there. The noun alone does not make the focus’s spring paradisal or life-giving.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"55:48\",\"ar\":\"ذَوَاتَآ أَفْنَانٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"55:50\",\"ar\":\"فِيهِمَا عَيْنَانِ تَجْرِيَانِ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"فِى عَيْنٍ حَمِئَةٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s explicit adjective and setting distinguish this spring from the flowing springs in 55:50.\",\"limits\":\"This contrast does not establish that the focus’s spring is dry, harmful, or literally where the sun enters the earth.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-55:66\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The paradise springs in 55:66 are described as gushing, a marked contrast with the focus’s muddy or hot spring at the sunset scene.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"55:66\",\"ar\":\"فِيهِمَا عَيْنَانِ نَضَّاخَتَانِ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"فِى عَيْنٍ حَمِئَةٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"The different spring descriptions are activated by the focus’s عين حمئة.\",\"limits\":\"The comparison distinguishes scene and quality; it does not imply that the focus’s spring is the opposite of paradise in every respect.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-56:4\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-56:18\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-56:22\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"wrong\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-56:83\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"same-as:R-75:26\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-57:25\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"57:25 joins revealed balance for justice with iron’s force and benefits. In Surah 18, Dhu al-Qarnayn is given a choice between punishment and goodness and later builds with iron, linking public power to moral judgment and practical protection.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"57:25\",\"ar\":\"وَأَنزَلْنَا مَعَهُمُ ٱلْكِتَٰبَ وَٱلْمِيزَانَ لِيَقُومَ ٱلنَّاسُ بِٱلْقِسْطِ ۖ وَأَنزَلْنَا ٱلْحَدِيدَ فِيهِ بَأْسٌۭ شَدِيدٌۭ وَمَنَٰفِعُ لِلنَّاسِ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:96\",\"ar\":\"ءَاتُونِى زُبَرَ ٱلْحَدِيدِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus makes the ruler’s use of power explicit, while the same surah later gives him iron for a protective barrier.\",\"limits\":\"57:25 does not name Dhu al-Qarnayn or identify his barrier with the iron’s stated force in that verse.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-68:39\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-70:1\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-72:15\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The jinn passage divides its group into Muslims and القاسطون, then gives them sharply different directions and outcomes; it is a thin parallel to the focus’s binary treatment followed by moral sorting.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"72:14\",\"ar\":\"وَأَنَّا مِنَّا ٱلْمُسْلِمُونَ وَمِنَّا ٱلْقَٰسِطُونَ ۖ فَمَنْ أَسْلَمَ فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ تَحَرَّوْا۟ رَشَدًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"72:15\",\"ar\":\"وَأَمَّا ٱلْقَٰسِطُونَ فَكَانُوا۟ لِجَهَنَّمَ حَطَبًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s two alternatives are resolved by the next verses into categories of wrongdoing and faith.\",\"limits\":\"The jinn report classifies themselves; it does not address a ruler choosing how to govern others.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-72:22\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-72:28\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-73:20\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-75:9\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-75:26\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The focus and the death scenes in 75:26 and 56:83 use a بلغ threshold within an إذا frame before the scene changes: a reached western limit leads to an encounter, while bodily limits bring observers and speech. This may be a wider Quranic threshold pattern.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ مَغْرِبَ ٱلشَّمْسِ\"},{\"ref\":\"75:26\",\"ar\":\"كَلَّآ إِذَا بَلَغَتِ ٱلتَّرَاقِىَ\"},{\"ref\":\"56:83\",\"ar\":\"فَلَوْلَآ إِذَا بَلَغَتِ ٱلْحُلْقُومَ\"}],\"missing\":\"The shared construction and terminal images are present, but further Quranic examples or rhetorical evidence would be needed to show a deliberate threshold pattern rather than a common way to describe arrival.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-81:1\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-88:5\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"88:5 offers a possible contrast for the focus if حَمِئَة is read with hot-spring pressure: both passages place an intense water source in an عين, but the focus’s adjective is textually different.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"فِى عَيْنٍ حَمِئَةٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"88:5\",\"ar\":\"تُسْقَىٰ مِنْ عَيْنٍ ءَانِيَةٍۢ\"}],\"missing\":\"The verses do not establish that the focus’s adjective means hot. A variant-reading source or lexical evidence relating حَمِئَة to heat could make this comparison decisive; without it, the shared spring image is only suggestive.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-88:12\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"same-as:R-55:50\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-91:1\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-102:2\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-102:7\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"wrong\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-104:4\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-f-حسن-عذب-عند\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The formula family sets حسن beside عذاب with different claimants and expectations: 9:52 has believers await one of two good outcomes while their opponents face punishment, and 41:50 shows a speaker falsely presuming حسن from God before severe punishment. In the focus, the ruler’s options are then assigned by wrongdoing or faith.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"9:52\",\"ar\":\"إِحْدَى ٱلْحُسْنَيَيْنِ ۖ وَنَحْنُ نَتَرَبَّصُ بِكُمْ أَن يُصِيبَكُمُ ٱللَّهُ بِعَذَابٍۢ مِّنْ عِندِهِۦٓ أَوْ بِأَيْدِينَا\"},{\"ref\":\"41:50\",\"ar\":\"وَلَئِن رُّجِعْتُ إِلَىٰ رَبِّىٓ إِنَّ لِى عِندَهُۥ لَلْحُسْنَىٰ\"},{\"ref\":\"41:50\",\"ar\":\"وَلَنُذِيقَنَّهُم مِّنْ عَذَابٍ غَلِيظٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:88\",\"ar\":\"وَأَمَّا مَنْ ءَامَنَ وَعَمِلَ صَٰلِحًۭا فَلَهُۥ جَزَآءً ٱلْحُسْنَىٰ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus pairs تُعَذِّبَ with حُسْنًا, and 18:87–88 specify who receives each outcome.\",\"limits\":\"These passages do not share the focus’s ruler or exact construction; the comparison concerns how goodness and punishment are allocated.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-f-ءخذ-عذب-وجد\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Surah 18’s 58th verse says divine seizure and punishment are deferred to an appointed time and that the people will find no refuge; 18:87 distinguishes Dhu al-Qarnayn’s local punishment from the later punishment when the wrongdoer returns to his Lord.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:58\",\"ar\":\"لَوْ يُؤَاخِذُهُم بِمَا كَسَبُوا۟ لَعَجَّلَ لَهُمُ ٱلْعَذَابَ ۚ بَل لَّهُم مَّوْعِدٌۭ لَّن يَجِدُوا۟ مِن دُونِهِۦ مَوْئِلًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:87\",\"ar\":\"فَسَوْفَ نُعَذِّبُهُۥ ثُمَّ يُرَدُّ إِلَىٰ رَبِّهِۦ فَيُعَذِّبُهُۥ عَذَابًۭا نُّكْرًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s punishment option is taken up in the next verse, which explicitly separates present authority from the return to God.\",\"limits\":\"18:58 speaks about God’s deferred punishment generally, not Dhu al-Qarnayn’s specific encounter.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-f-بلغ-حسن-عند\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"17:23 links an arrival threshold at a child’s home with old age to required kindness and gentle speech; it is a thin ethical parallel to the focus’s arrival and choice of treatment. Its إِمَّا is conditional, unlike the focus’s paired either-or construction.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"17:23\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّا يَبْلُغَنَّ عِندَكَ ٱلْكِبَرَ أَحَدُهُمَآ أَوْ كِلَاهُمَا فَلَا تَقُل لَّهُمَآ أُفٍّۢ وَلَا تَنْهَرْهُمَا وَقُل لَّهُمَا قَوْلًۭا كَرِيمًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages make a reaching or encounter frame relevant to how people are treated.\",\"limits\":\"The focus’s people are not identified as vulnerable kin, and the two uses of إِمَّا have different grammatical force.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-f-ءخذ-عذب\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-f-ءخذ-حسن\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"2:229 presents a constrained binary in a human relationship: retain with recognized conduct or release with beneficence. It gives a Quranic parallel for making humane treatment an explicit branch of an authority’s choice.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"2:229\",\"ar\":\"فَإِمْسَاكٌۢ بِمَعْرُوفٍ أَوْ تَسْرِيحٌۢ بِإِحْسَٰنٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:88\",\"ar\":\"وَأَمَّا مَنْ ءَامَنَ وَعَمِلَ صَٰلِحًۭا فَلَهُۥ جَزَآءً ٱلْحُسْنَىٰ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus pairs punishment with حسنًا, and its following verses make the humane branch concrete for the believer.\",\"limits\":\"The divorce ruling is a different legal setting and does not determine the precise sense of the focus’s حسنًا.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-f-ءخذ-عند\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-f-حسن-عند\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus’s حسنًا names goodness adopted among people; 3:195 places حسن الثواب with God, and 18:88 then names جزاء الحسنى for the believer. The sequence can connect humane conduct in the scene to ultimate recompense.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"3:195\",\"ar\":\"ثَوَابًۭا مِّنْ عِندِ ٱللَّهِ ۗ وَٱللَّهُ عِندَهُۥ حُسْنُ ٱلثَّوَابِ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:88\",\"ar\":\"فَلَهُۥ جَزَآءً ٱلْحُسْنَىٰ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s حسنًا is followed immediately by a verse promising جزاء الحسنى to the one who believes and acts rightly.\",\"limits\":\"The verses do not state that the ruler’s حسنًا is itself the afterlife reward; the link is a shared goodness register across conduct and recompense.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-f-ءخذ-وجد\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Surah 18 repeats a found-then-take sequence: after finding a wall, Moses says Khidr could have taken wages for repairing it; at the later encounter, the offered choice is punishment or adopting goodness among the people. Dhu al-Qarnayn later declines tribute, reinforcing the contrast between service and extraction.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:77\",\"ar\":\"فَوَجَدَا فِيهَا جِدَارًۭا يُرِيدُ أَن يَنقَضَّ فَأَقَامَهُۥ ۖ قَالَ لَوْ شِئْتَ لَتَّخَذْتَ عَلَيْهِ أَجْرًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"وَوَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا ۗ قُلْنَا يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:95\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ مَا مَكَّنِّى فِيهِ رَبِّى خَيْرٌۭ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s two وَجَدَ clauses lead to an option involving اتخذ, as does the earlier wall episode.\",\"limits\":\"The same roots do not make the actions identical: اتخذ أجرا concerns wages, while اتخذ فيهم حسنا concerns treatment.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-f-حسن-عذب\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"2:178 places pardon and a measured settlement within the law of retaliation, then warns against transgression. That makes the focus’s حسنًا branch legible as a constructive alternative within an authority frame that still distinguishes justice from wrongdoing.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"2:178\",\"ar\":\"فَمَنْ عُفِىَ لَهُۥ مِنْ أَخِيهِ شَىْءٌۭ فَٱتِّبَاعٌۢ بِٱلْمَعْرُوفِ وَأَدَآءٌ إِلَيْهِ بِإِحْسَٰنٍۢ ۗ ذَٰلِكَ تَخْفِيفٌۭ مِّن رَّبِّكُمْ وَرَحْمَةٌۭ ۗ فَمَنِ ٱعْتَدَىٰ بَعْدَ ذَٰلِكَ فَلَهُۥ عَذَابٌ أَلِيمٌۭ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:87\",\"ar\":\"أَمَّا مَن ظَلَمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus offers punishment or goodness, and the following verse identifies wrongdoing as the basis for punishment.\",\"limits\":\"2:178 concerns retaliation and a victim’s kin; it is an analogy for bounded clemency, not a rule for the people in the focus.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-f-عذب-عند\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"6:124 locates severe punishment with God, while 18:87 distinguishes Dhu al-Qarnayn’s punishment from the later punishment when the wrongdoer returns to his Lord. The family may sharpen the boundary between local rule and divine judgment.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"6:124\",\"ar\":\"سَيُصِيبُ ٱلَّذِينَ أَجْرَمُوا۟ صَغَارٌ عِندَ ٱللَّهِ وَعَذَابٌۭ شَدِيدٌۢ بِمَا كَانُوا۟ يَمْكُرُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:87\",\"ar\":\"فَسَوْفَ نُعَذِّبُهُۥ ثُمَّ يُرَدُّ إِلَىٰ رَبِّهِۦ فَيُعَذِّبُهُۥ عَذَابًۭا نُّكْرًۭا\"}],\"missing\":\"The focus does not use عند الله with its local punishment; 18:87 supplies a return-to-the-Lord distinction, but a direct usage parallel would be needed to establish that this formula family shapes the reading.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-f-عذب-وجد\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-f-عند-وجد\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"In the earlier journey episode, Moses and his companion find a servant who has been given mercy and knowledge from God; in the focus, Dhu al-Qarnayn finds a people and receives a divine address about how to treat them. In Surah 18, finding can open a divinely guided encounter rather than merely mark location.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:65\",\"ar\":\"فَوَجَدَا عَبْدًۭا مِّنْ عِبَادِنَآ ءَاتَيْنَٰهُ رَحْمَةًۭ مِّنْ عِندِنَا وَعَلَّمْنَٰهُ مِن لَّدُنَّا عِلْمًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"وَوَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا ۗ قُلْنَا يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both travel narratives use وَجَدَ at a turning point followed by knowledge or instruction from God.\",\"limits\":\"The two عند phrases differ: one means from Us, the other locates people near the scene; the narrative parallel does not make their grammar equivalent.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-f-شمس-غرب\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"In 2:258, bringing the sun from the west defeats a ruler’s claim to sovereign power. That offers a contrast for Dhu al-Qarnayn’s reached مغرب الشمس: he exercises authority over people, while the sun’s course remains a sign of power beyond human control.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"2:258\",\"ar\":\"فَإِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يَأْتِى بِٱلشَّمْسِ مِنَ ٱلْمَشْرِقِ فَأْتِ بِهَا مِنَ ٱلْمَغْرِبِ فَبُهِتَ ٱلَّذِى كَفَرَ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ مَغْرِبَ ٱلشَّمْسِ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"قُلْنَا يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus places a powerful ruler at the sun’s western setting and then defines his authority as a choice over the people there.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not say Dhu al-Qarnayn controls the sun or explicitly invoke Abraham’s debate; this is a contrast in the scope of human and divine power.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"X1\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The next two verses resolve the focus’s options rather than leaving them open: أما من ظلم receives punishment from Dhu al-Qarnayn and then from his Lord, while وأما من آمن وعمل صالحا receives الحسنى and ease. The choice is governed by conduct, not arbitrary preference.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:87\",\"ar\":\"أَمَّا مَن ظَلَمَ فَسَوْفَ نُعَذِّبُهُۥ ثُمَّ يُرَدُّ إِلَىٰ رَبِّهِۦ فَيُعَذِّبُهُۥ عَذَابًۭا نُّكْرًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:88\",\"ar\":\"وَأَمَّا مَنْ ءَامَنَ وَعَمِلَ صَٰلِحًۭا فَلَهُۥ جَزَآءً ٱلْحُسْنَىٰ ۖ وَسَنَقُولُ لَهُۥ مِنْ أَمْرِنَا يُسْرًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The repeated اما in 18:87–88 answers the paired إما in the focus and specifies the grounds and consequences of each branch.\",\"limits\":\"This continuation makes the moral criteria clear, but does not identify the people’s individual deeds beyond those categories.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}"}}
